# AWK
AWK is a powerful Linux command-line tool used for text processing, pattern searching, and data extraction from files or command output.
It works line by line and is mostly used for handling structured data like tables, logs, and CSV files.

>Main Uses of AWK
Searching specific patterns in text
Extracting columns from files
Performing calculations
Generating formatted reports
Processing large text files
Example
awk '{print $1}' file.txt

This prints the first column of every line in file.txt.

>Features
Supports variables and loops
Works like a small programming language
Fast for text/data manipulation
Useful for automation and scripting

# SED
SED (Stream Editor) is a Linux command used to edit and modify text automatically in files or command output without opening the file manually.

It processes text line by line and is mainly used for searching, replacing, deleting, and editing text.

>Main Uses of SED
Find and replace words
Delete lines
Insert new text
Modify file content automatically
Batch text editing
Example
sed 's/hello/hi/g' file.txt

This replaces all occurrences of hello with hi.

>Features
Non-interactive text editor
Very useful in shell scripting
Fast processing for large files
Supports regular expressions

# GREP
GREP is a Linux command used to search specific words, patterns, or expressions inside files and display matching lines.

It is mainly used for filtering and finding required information from large amounts of text.

>Main Uses of GREP
Search words in files
Find patterns using regular expressions
Filter command output
Search logs and error messages
Case-sensitive or case-insensitive search
Example
grep "error" file.txt

This displays all lines containing the word error.

>Features
Fast text searching
Supports regular expressions
Useful for log analysis
Can search multiple files at once

